¶
'''2.''' Growing trees, yielding wood suitable for constructive uses; trees generally; woods. See ''timber-tree.''

{{smaller block|The old ash, the oak, and other ''timber'' shewed no signs of winter.
{{right|''Gray'', Letters, I. 247.}}}}

'''3.''' In ''British law'', the kind of tree which a tenant for life may not cut; in general, oak, ash, and elm of the age of twenty years and upward, unless so old as not to have a reasonable quantity of useful wood in them, the limit being, according to some authorities, enough to make a good post. {{smaller|Local customs include also (''a'') some other trees, such as beech or hornbeam, and (''b'') trees of less or greater age or tested by girth instead of age.}}

'''4.''' Stuff; material.

{{smaller block|They are the fittest ''timber'' to make great politics of.
{{right|''Bacon'', Goodness (ed. 1887).}}}}

'''5.''' A single piece of wood, either suitable for use in some construction or already in such use; a beam, either by itself or forming a member of any structure: as, the ''timbers'' of a house or of a bridge.—'''6.''' ''Naut.'', one of the curving pieces of wood branching upward from the keel of a vessel, forming the ribs.—'''7.''' The wooden part of something, as the beam or handle of a spear.

{{smaller block|He bowed on his horse nekke, and the ''tymbir'' of the speres fly in peces.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 541.}}}}

'''8.''' The stocks. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The squire . . . gives me over to the beadle, who claps me here in the ''timber''.
{{right|''D. Jerrold'', Men of Character, Christopher Snub, i.}}}}
